{
  "query": "A mobile gaming studio plans to add in app messaging so players can exchange text, photos, and short clips in real time. The platform must scale to more than 25 million monthly users with under 100 milliseconds for end to end message delivery and it must support flexible schemas for player profiles, conversation threads, and channel metadata across web and mobile clients. Which managed database on Google Cloud best fits these requirements?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Cloud Spanner",
      "explanation": "A globally distributed relational database that offers strong consistency and horizontal scaling",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Firestore in Datastore mode",
      "explanation": "A document database that uses the Datastore API and targets server centric workloads without real time listeners",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Firestore in Native mode",
      "explanation": "A document database for mobile and web apps that provides real time sync and a flexible schema",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Bigtable",
      "explanation": "A wide column NoSQL store optimized for massive throughput and low latency key value access",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p><b>Firestore in Native mode</b> is the correct choice.</p><p><b>Firestore</b> provides real time listeners for web and mobile clients so messages and presence updates propagate in near real time. It uses a flexible document model that cleanly represents player profiles, conversation threads, and channel metadata. <b>Firestore</b> automatically scales to millions of users with high write throughput and offers multi region configurations that help keep read and write latency low. Its client SDKs and offline support are designed for mobile and web applications which makes building a responsive in app messaging experience straightforward.</p><p><i>Cloud Spanner</i> offers global consistency and horizontal scaling but it is a relational database with a rigid schema and it does not provide real time client listeners for web and mobile apps. It is better suited for transactional relational workloads rather than chat style document data with rapidly changing conversations.</p><p><i>Firestore in Datastore mode</i> lacks the real time client SDKs and live query listeners that are needed for instant messaging experiences. It targets server side access patterns and does not provide the same mobile and web developer features that make chat apps simple to build.</p><p><i>Bigtable</i> delivers very low latency at massive scale but it is a wide column store that requires careful row key design and it does not offer flexible queries or native real time listeners to clients. It is more appropriate for time series and analytical workloads than for user centric messaging documents and threads.</p>",
